Presidential elections were held in [[Poland]] on {{date|2025-05-18|DMY}}. As no candidate received a majority of the vote, a second round was held on {{date|2025-06-01|DMY}}. Incumbent president [[Andrzej Duda]] was ineligible for re-election to a third term. The second round was won by conservative [[Institute of National Remembrance]] director [[Karol Nawrocki]], with 50.89% of the vote, who was backed by the [[Law and Justice]] (PiS) party. Nawrocki defeated the progressive-liberal [[Mayor of Warsaw]], [[Rafał Trzaskowski]], who received 49.11% of the vote, [[2024 Civic Coalition presidential primary|nominated]] [[2020 Polish presidential election|for the second time]] by the [[Civic Coalition (Poland)|Civic Coalition]] (KO). It was the third consecutive victory for a candidate supported by Law and Justice in the presidential elections.

In the first round, Trzaskowski narrowly came first with 31.4% of the vote, while the right-wing and far-right candidates, Nawrocki, [[Sławomir Mentzen]] ([[Confederation Liberty and Independence|Confederation]]) and [[Grzegorz Braun]] ([[Confederation of the Polish Crown|KKP]]) overperformed polls, winning 29.5%, 14.8% and 6.3% respectively, coming in second, third and fourth. Other candidates of the ruling coalition underperformed and fell below expectations; the centre-right candidate [[Szymon Hołownia]] ([[Poland 2050|PL2050]]) received 4.99% of the vote while the left-wing candidates together secured 10.2%, with coalition candidate [[Magdalena Biejat]] ([[The Left (Poland)|The Left]]) coming below opposition [[Adrian Zandberg]] ([[Razem]]).

Nawrocki ran on a nationalist and [[Social conservatism|socially conservative]] platform, focusing his campaign against [[Third Cabinet of Donald Tusk|the incumbent government]]. Nawrocki's platform called for [[Economic patriotism|significant government intervention in the economy]], close ties between the [[Catholic Church in Poland|Catholic Church]] and the [[Politics of Poland|Polish government]], opposition to the [[European Green Deal]] and economic regulations, the [[Abortion in Poland|maintenance of Poland's restrictive abortion laws]], also being in opposition to legalization of [[same-sex marriage]] or [[civil union]]s. Trzaskowski supported moderate [[economic liberalization]], social [[liberalism]], [[Green politics|ecological regulations]], [[European integration]], the [[Abortion in Poland|broad legalization of abortion]], the introduction of [[same sex civil union|same-sex civil union]]s, and a [[decentralization|greater role]] for the local governments of [[Voivodeships of Poland|voivodeships]]. They also differed on their foreign policy approach, with Trzaskowski supporting the further strengthening of relations with the [[European Union]] and [[Ukraine–NATO relations|approving Ukraine's membership in NATO]], and Nawrocki opposing Ukraine's accession to NATO and being against the strengthening of relations with the EU, instead supporting stronger cooperation with the [[United States]]. Both, however, supported continuing military support to Ukraine.{{refn|<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-05-22/polish-presidential-hopeful-says-opposes-ukraine-joining-nato|title=Polish Presidential Candidate Rules Out Ukraine Joining NATO|website=bloomberg.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pravda.com.ua/eng/news/2025/05/23/7513646/|title=Polish PM on presidential candidate's promise not to let Ukraine into NATO: It's treason|website=Ukrainska Pravda}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=24 November 2024 |title=Polish opposition party chooses historian Karol Nawrocki as presidential candidate |url=https://www.euronews.com/my-europe/2024/11/24/polish-opposition-party-chooses-historian-karol-nawrocki-as-presidential-candidate |website=[[Euronews]] |language=pl}}</ref>}}

Observers noted that a victory for Nawrocki would hurt [[Donald Tusk]]'s government due the governing coalition not having votes in the [[Sejm]] to overrule a presidential [[veto]].<ref>{{bulletlist|{{Cite web |date={{date|2025-05-16|DMY}} |title=Poland's presidential election a crucial test for Tusk's pro-European government |url=https://www.eunews.it/en/2025/05/16/polands-presidential-election-a-crucial-test-for-tusks-pro-european-government/ |access-date=2025-06-02 |language=en-US}}|{{Cite web |title=Poland's presidential election set for tight vote |url=https://www.rte.ie/news/europe/2025/0531/1516016-polish-election/ |access-date=2025-06-02 |website=amp.rte.ie|date={{date|2025-05-31|DMY}} |last1=Nolan |first1=Liam}}|{{Cite web |last=Brzozowski |first=Alexandra |date={{date|2025-06-02|DMY}} |title=Polish presidential result spells trouble for Tusk |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/politics/news/polish-presidential-result-spells-trouble-for-tusk/ |access-date=2 June 2025 |website=Euractiv}}|{{Cite news |last1=Krupa |first1=Jakub |last2=Walker |first2=Shaun |date={{date|2025-06-02|DMY}} |title=Poland: right-wing candidate Karol Nawrocki ahead in presidential election runoff |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jun/01/exit-poll-in-polish-presidential-run-off-puts-candidates-neck-and-neck |access-date=2025-06-02 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}|{{Cite web |date={{date|2025-06-02|DMY}} |last=Aitken |first=Peter |title=Donald Trump-backed candidate wins Poland's presidential election |url=https://www.newsweek.com/poland-exit-polls-show-donald-trum-backed-candidate-narrowly-2079594 |access-date=2025-06-02 |website=Newsweek |language=en}}}}</ref> The election result continued the trend of tighter electoral margins over the last 25 years, becoming the closest in Polish history, and the streak of Law and Justice aligned presidential candidates winning presidential elections, losing only [[2010 Polish presidential election|one]] out of five since its founding in 2001. The first-round results indicated a notable shift in political momentum for [[anti-establishment]] [[List of political parties in Poland|parties]], with the [[Confederation Liberty and Independence]] (Mentzen), [[Confederation of the Polish Crown]] (Braun) and [[Razem]] (Zandberg) having their best results in history. Exit polls indicated that both candidates won nearly 50% of each age demographic, a change from older voters voting for the Law and Justice candidate and younger voters voting for Trzaskowski in the prior election.<ref>{{cite news |date={{date|2025-06-01|DMY}} |title= Jak głosowali najmłodsi wyborcy, a jak seniorzy?  |url=https://www.infor.pl/prawo/nowosci-prawne/6961109,jak-glosowali-najmlodsi-wyborcy-a-jak-seniorzy.html |access-date=2025-06-22}}</ref> Anti-establishment parties gained most of the youth vote; the far-right Sławomir Mentzen performed best among the youngest generation of Polish voters, with left-wing Adrian Zandberg coming second.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sorgho |first=Olivier |date=2025-05-30 |title=Disillusioned with political mainstream, young Poles turn to far right and left |url=https://notesfrompoland.com/2025/05/30/disillusioned-with-the-political-mainstream-young-polish-voters-turn-to-far-right-and-left/ |access-date=2025-06-02 |website=Notes From Poland |language=en-US}}</ref> The election saw the largest number of candidates since the [[1995 Polish presidential election|1995 presidential election]], with 13 candidates running for president.

== Electoral system ==
{{multiple image
| align             = right
| perrow            = 2
| total_width       = 400
| image1            = Karta głosowania wybory prezydenckie 2025 I tura.jpg
| image2            = Karta do głosowania w wyborach Prezydenta Rzeczypospolitej Polskiej w dniu 1 czerwca 2025 r.jpg
| footer            = Voting ballots in the first (left) and second (right) rounds of the election.
|}}
Presidential elections in Poland must be held on a day off work (Sunday or public holiday), between 75 and 100 days before the end of the term of the sitting president. However, they may be held earlier if the office becomes vacant due to the death, resignation, or removal of the incumbent.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Article 128, paragraph 2 of the Constitution of the Republic of Poland of 2 April 1997 (Journal of Laws No. 78, item 483) |url=https://www.sejm.gov.pl/prawo/konst/polski/5.htm |access-date=2025-06-13 |website=www.sejm.gov.pl}}</ref> The [[Marshal of the Sejm]] is responsible for setting the date of presidential elections and in this case had three possible dates to choose from: 4 May, 11 May, or 18 May 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-04-09 |title=Wybory prezydenckie w Polsce 2025. Najważniejsze daty w kalendarzu wyborczym |url=https://wiadomosci.onet.pl/wybory/wybory-prezydenckie/wybory-prezydenckie-2025-kalendarz-wyborczy/qx921b2 |access-date=2025-06-03 |website=Onet Wiadomości |language=pl}}</ref>

The [[President of Poland]] is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, a run-off is held between the top two candidates. Presidents serve a five-year term and can be re-elected once. Presidential terms expire on 6 August,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-06-01 |title=Kiedy koniec kadencji Andrzeja Dudy? Data nie jest przypadkowa |url=https://www.tokfm.pl/Tokfm/7,103087,31938633,kiedy-andrzej-duda-konczy-prezydenture-data-zaprzysiezenia.html |access-date=2025-07-23 |website=TOK FM |language=pl}}</ref> and the president-elect takes the oath of office on the same day before the [[National Assembly (Poland)|National Assembly]] (a joint session of the [[Sejm]] and the [[Senate of Poland|Senate]]).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Senate of the Republic of Poland / About the Senate / The Constitution / Chapter V |url=https://www.senat.gov.pl/en/about-the-senate/konstytucja/chapter-v/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230714201749/https://www.senat.gov.pl/en/about-the-senate/konstytucja/chapter-v/ |archive-date=2023-07-14 |access-date=2025-07-23 |website=www.senat.gov.pl |language=en}}</ref>
[[File:OKW nr 94 i 95 w Szczecinie, 2025.jpg|thumb|left|A district electoral commission in [[Szczecin]]]]

In order to be registered to contest the election, a candidate must be a Polish citizen, be at least 35 years old on the day of the first round of the election, and have collected at least 100,000 voters' signatures by {{date|2025-04-04|DMY}} at 16:00 ([[Central European Standard Time|CEST]]).<ref name=kalendarzwyborczy>{{cite web |title=Wybory Prezydenta Rzeczypospolitej Polskiej 2025 r. |url=https://wybory.gov.pl/prezydent2025/en/kalendarz |access-date=2025-01-16 |website=wybory.gov.pl |language=pl}}</ref> In 2025, 13 candidates registered, the highest amount of candidates tied with [[1995 Polish presidential election|1995]].

All citizens are eligible to vote following their 18th birthday, except for those that have been [[disenfranchisement|disenfranchised]], stripped of public rights, or are [[detainment|detained]]. Voters cast votes in their regional district electoral commissions (plural {{langx|pl|okręgowe komisje wyborcze}}), but can also vote abroad, outside of their assigned district electoral commission, or [[voting by mail|by correspondence]] if they have notified the electoral commission beforehand.<ref name=kalendarzwyborczy/><ref>{{cite web |title=Najczęściej zadawane pytania – wybory Prezydenta RP 2025 |trans-title=Frequently asked questions – elections for President of Poland 2025 |url=https://wybory.gov.pl/prezydent2025/en/pkw_aktualnosci/69458 |access-date=2025-05-21}}</ref> Voting takes place for 14 hours, between 7:00 and 21:00 (CEST).<ref>{{cite news |title= Wybory prezydenckie 2025. Godziny otwarcia lokali wyborczych |url=https://www.gazetaprawna.pl/wiadomosci/artykuly/9799416,wybory-prezydenckie-2025-godziny-otwarcia-lokali-wyborczych.html |access-date=2025-05-23}}</ref>

The elections are managed by the [[National Electoral Commission (Poland)|National Electoral Commission]] ({{langx|pl|Państwowa Komisja Wyborcza}}), which, for this election, was composed of the chairman ({{ill|Sylwester Marciniak|pl}}), deputy chairman ({{ill|Wojciech Sych|pl}}) and seven members recommended by groups in the [[Sejm]] — two by [[Civic Coalition (Poland)|Civic Coalition]], two by [[Law and Justice]], and one respectively by [[Poland 2050]], the [[Polish People's Party]], and [[The Left (Poland)|The Left]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Matłacz |first=Agnieszka |date=2023-12-22 |title=Sejm wybrał siedmiu członków Państwowej Komisji Wyborczej |url=https://www.prawo.pl/samorzad/sklad-pkw-2023-2027,524613.html |publisher=Prawo.pl |access-date=2025-01-10}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |last1=Chmielnicki |first1=Marcin |date={{date|2024-03-06|DMY}} |title=Prezydent RP powołał członków Państwowej Komisji Wyborczej |trans-title=The Polish President has appointed the members of the National Electoral Commission |url=https://pkw.gov.pl/aktualnosci/informacje/prezydent-rp-powolal-czlonkow-panstwowej-komisji-wyborczej |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240310125412/https://pkw.gov.pl/aktualnosci/informacje/prezydent-rp-powolal-czlonkow-panstwowej-komisji-wyborczej |archive-date={{date|2024-03-10|DMY}} |publisher=[[National Electoral Commission (Poland)|Państwowa Komisja Wyborcza]] |access-date={{date|2025-05-30|DMY}} |language=pl}}</ref>

The National Electoral Commission, in press conferences throughout election day, reports turnout for 12:00, 17:00 (CEST) and the final turnout.<ref>{{cite news |title=Frekwencja o godz. 12: 20,28 proc. Mniej niż w ostatnich wyborach prezydenckich i parlamentarnych |url=https://oko.press/frekwencja-o-godz-12-2028-proc-mniej-niz-w-ostatnich-wyborach-prezydenckich-i-parlamentarnych |access-date=2025-05-03}}</ref> For the day preceding, and day of, the election, until polls close at 21:00, [[election silence]] is in place. In the second round of the presidential election, there were 87 incidents of electoral silence being broken.<ref>{{cite web |date={{date|2025-06-01|DMY}} |last=Szczerbiak |first=Agata |title=Wyniki exit poll na żyletki: Trzaskowski nieznacznie przed Nawrockim. Napięcie w obu sztabach |url=https://www.polityka.pl/tygodnikpolityka/kraj/2302461,1,wyniki-exit-poll-na-zyletki-trzaskowski-nieznacznie-przed-nawrockim-napiecie-w-obu-sztabach.read |access-date=2025-06-01}}</ref>

== Background ==
=== Duda's second inauguration ===
[[File:Uroczystość zaprzysiężenia Prezydenta RP Andrzeja Dudy przed Zgromadzeniem Narodowym (50195304887).jpg|thumb|Andrzej Duda being sworn in for his second term on {{date|2020-08-06|DMY}}]]
Incumbent President [[Andrzej Duda]] ([[Law and Justice|PiS]]) narrowly defeated [[Rafał Trzaskowski]] ([[Civic Platform|PO]]) in the [[2020 Polish presidential election|2020 presidential election]] and was sworn in for his second term on {{date|2020-08-06|DMY}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=Prezydent RP Andrzej Duda zaprzysiężony na drugą kadencję |url=https://www.gov.pl/web/uw-mazowiecki/prezydent-rp-andrzej-duda-zaprzysiezony-na-druga-kadencje |access-date=2025-05-21}}</ref> Duda would govern along with [[Prime Minister of Poland|Prime Minister]] [[Mateusz Morawiecki]] and [[Second Cabinet of Mateusz Morawiecki|his cabinet]] until the [[2023 Polish parliamentary election|2023 parliamentary election]]. The parliamentary election saw record-high turnout, with 74.4% of eligible Poles casting their vote, an increase of 12.6 percentage points since [[2019 Polish parliamentary election|2019]].

=== Tusk's cabinet ===
[[File:Third Government of Donald Tusk.jpg|thumb|right|350px|Third Cabinet of Donald Tusk]]
[[File:Umowa koalicyjna crop.png|thumb|350px|right|Leaders of coalition parties signing the coalition agreement, left to right: [[Szymon Hołownia]] ([[Poland 2050|PL2050]]-[[Third Way (Poland)|TD]]), [[Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz]] ([[Polish People's Party|PSL]]-[[Third Way (Poland)|TD]]), Donald Tusk ([[Civic Coalition (Poland)|KO]]), [[Włodzimierz Czarzasty]] ([[New Left (Poland)|NL]]) and [[Robert Biedroń]] ([[New Left (Poland)|NL]]).<br> In the presidential election, the TD alliance fielded Szymon Hołownia, KO fielded [[Rafał Trzaskowski]], and NL fielded [[Magdalena Biejat]].]]
Following the [[2023 Polish parliamentary election|2023 parliamentary election]] and installment of the short-lived [[Third Cabinet of Mateusz Morawiecki|renomination]] of Mateusz Morawiecki, [[Third Cabinet of Donald Tusk|Donald Tusk's cabinet]], comprising [[Civic Coalition (Poland)|Civic Coalition]], [[Poland 2050]], the [[Polish People's Party]], and the [[New Left (Poland)|New Left]], began governing the country. Tusk's coalition did not have enough votes to bypass the presidential veto, for which it would need 276 votes.<ref>{{cite news |date=2023-10-17 |title=Ile głosów potrzeba, by odrzucić weto prezydenta? Opozycja ociera się o tę liczbę |url=https://wiadomosci.onet.pl/kraj/ile-glosow-potrzeba-aby-odrzucic-weto-prezydenta-wyniki-wyborow-2023/ccj1plz |access-date=2024-12-14}}</ref>

==== Tusk cabinet economic policy ====
Since the coalition's rise to power, Tusk's cabinet had pledged to [[deregulation|deregulate the economy]] and [[balanced budget|balance the budget]]. In February 2025, Tusk invited billionaire [[Rafał Brzoska]] and [[Google]] CEO [[Sundar Pichai]]<ref>{{cite news |date=2025-02-13 |title=Tusk: Google może być częścią projektu deregulacji w Europie i Polsce |url=https://www.gazetaprawna.pl/wiadomosci/kraj/artykuly/9738129,tusk-google-moze-byc-czescia-projektu-deregulacji-w-europie-i-polsce.html |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref> to deregulate the Polish economy and cut [[labor rights|labor regulations]]. Tusk's proposal of Brzoska has led to media labelling him as the "Polish [[Elon Musk]]", but Brzoska later abandoned the project, citing poor cooperation.<ref>{{cite news |date=2025-02-13 |title=Jaki kraj, taki Elon Musk, czyli Tuskowy trumpizm na miarę naszych możliwości |url=https://oko.press/jaki-kraj-taki-elon-musk-czyli-tuskowy-trumpizm-na-miare-naszych-mozliwosci |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=2025-02-10 |title="Polski Elon Musk", trzy razy słowo na "i" oraz energetyka. Co powiedział Donald Tusk? |url=https://next.gazeta.pl/next/7,151003,31680453,polski-elon-musk-trzy-razy-slowo-na-i-oraz-energetyka.html |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=2025-02-10 |title=Rafał Brzoska jak Elon Musk wesprze rząd w deregulacji gospodarki. Donald Tusk mówi Żabką, chce uwolnić czas i zasoby przedsiębiorców |url=https://www.wiadomoscihandlowe.pl/gospodarka-i-finanse/donald-tusk-zarzadza-wielka-deregulacje-polskiego-biznesu-wystosowal-prosbe-do-rafala-brzoski-prosze-sie-za-to-wziac-2523395 |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Redakcja |date=2025-04-19 |title=Rafał Brzoska odchodzi z zespołu ds. deregulacji |url=https://dzienniknarodowy.pl/?p=14590 |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=Dziennik Narodowy |language=pl-PL}}</ref> Tusk has been accused of granting Google a [[monopoly]] over the [[Artificial Intelligence]] sector in Poland via his investment agreements with Pichai.<ref>{{cite news |date=2025-02-13 |title=Tusk ogłasza sukces. A naprawdę? Oddaje Google monopol na AI w Polsce |url=https://www.gazetaprawna.pl/wiadomosci/kraj/artykuly/9738237,tusk-oglasza-sukces-a-naprawde-oddaje-google-monopol-na-ai-w-polsce.html |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref> In May, Tusk also promised more than one hundred laws that would help deregulate the Polish economy, but they were scrapped.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Premier zapowiada, że do końca maja gotowych będzie ponad 100 ustaw deregulacyjnych {{!}} Biznes PAP |url=https://biznes.pap.pl/wiadomosci/gospodarka/premier-zapowiada-ze-do-konca-maja-gotowych-bedzie-ponad-100-ustaw |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=biznes.pap.pl}}</ref>

==== Tusk cabinet social policy ====
The ruling coalition was composed of mostly centrist or slightly right leaning parties. However, the [[New Left (Poland)|New Left]] also being part of the cabinet, postulated decriminalization of abortion and progressive LGBT+-policies. The Sejm rejected the proposal in July 2024. Facing opposition within the ruling coalition of a large group of dissidents from the [[Polish People's Party]], the vote failed with 218 votes against and 215 for decriminalization.<ref>{{cite news |date=2024-07-12 |title=Koalicja rządząca przegrała głosowanie w sprawie aborcji. Kto jak głosował? |url=https://www.pap.pl/aktualnosci/koalicja-rzadzaca-przegrala-glosowanie-w-sprawie-aborcji-kto-jak-glosowal |access-date=2025-02-13}}</ref>

==== Tusk cabinet healthcare policy ====
On 4 April, the [[Sejm]] narrowly voted (213–190, with KO, PL2050 and PSL voting for – PiS, NL and Razem against – and Konfederacja mainly abstaining) to decrease the health insurance contribution<ref name=polskieradio>{{cite news |date=2025-05-06 |title=Polish president vetoes cut to entrepreneurs' health insurance contribution  |url=https://www.polskieradio.pl/395/7789/Artykul/3519717,polish-president-vetoes-cut-to-entrepreneurs%E2%80%99-health-insurance-contribution |quote=President Andrzej Duda on Tuesday vetoed a government-backed bill that would have cut the '''health insurance contribution''' rate for self-employed entrepreneurs,... |access-date=2025-05-17}}</ref> ({{langx|pl|składka zdrowotna}}) for entrepreneurs,<ref>{{cite news |title=Sejm przyjął obniżkę składki zdrowotnej. Lewica i PiS głosowały przeciwko |url=https://www.polityka.pl/tygodnikpolityka/kraj/2295681,1,sejm-przyjal-obnizke-skladki-zdrowotnej-lewica-i-pis-glosowaly-przeciwko.read |access-date=2025-05-17}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Sejm przegłosował niższą składkę zdrowotną. Rządowy projekt uratowała... Konfederacja |url=https://www.bankier.pl/wiadomosc/Sejm-przeglosowal-nizsza-skladke-zdrowotna-Rzadowy-projekt-uratowala-Konfederacja-8919666.html |access-date=2025-05-17}}</ref> which sparked protests from the left, especially members of [[Together Party|Razem]], accusing the government of attempting to undermine and then privatize public healthcare.<ref name="CC">{{cite news |title=Polish left-wing candidates urge president to veto health contribution cut |url=https://www.polskieradio.pl/395/11354/artykul/3510351,polish-leftwing-candidates-urge-president-to-veto-health-contribution-cut |access-date=2025-05-17}}</ref> Ultimately, Andrzej Duda vetoed the health contribution decrease on 6 May.<ref name=polskieradio/>

